    {
      "slug": "impossible-ceramics",
      "title": "Impossible ceramics",
      "modules": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Using an experimental 3D printing material, I developed a technique for crafting intricate geometries in porcelain and other ceramics"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "images": [
            {
              "url": "/images/1f353907-8905-4b0a-8492-dbfc2d11498c_rw_1920.jpg",
              "ratio": 89.67
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Ceramic filters for metal casting are often produced using synthetic or natural sponges. The sponge is dipped into liquid ceramic, which it absorbs. After drying, the sponge undergoes firing, during which the synthetic or organic material decomposes, leaving only the ceramic material present."
        },
        {
          "type": "gallery",
          "images": [
            {
              "url": "/images/97f5ba5b-1dde-4dd1-842d-828dcca2b7b5_rw_3840.jpg",
              "w": 3840,
              "h": 2880
            },
            {
              "url": "/images/6f1ceb5b-675a-4656-a657-7e41bddef98e_rw_3840.jpg",
              "w": 3840,
              "h": 2880
            },
            {
              "url": "/images/a2c1c681-31f0-49ae-8ce5-6c5ee3dd8561_rw_1920.jpg",
              "w": 1830,
              "h": 1565
            },
            {
              "url": "/images/67012cf1-d9d0-4451-adf1-f74de8c893a8_rw_3840.jpg",
              "w": 3840,
              "h": 2880
            },
            {
              "url": "/images/a4183fa6-64c0-4ad2-a156-163a1e2335de_rw_3840.jpg",
              "w": 3840,
              "h": 2880
            },
            {
              "url": "/images/7c8ebf45-903a-480d-b714-a314ef43bb1a_rw_3840.jpg",
              "w": 3840,
              "h": 2160
            },
            {
              "url": "/images/6ff23820-d46f-45c3-a94d-286b49aa221a_rw_600.gif",
              "w": 600,
              "h": 1067
            },
            {
              "url": "/images/eb5a70cc-6938-4a6d-af00-e9d79c18ea17_rw_3840.jpg",
              "w": 3840,
              "h": 2880
            },
            {
              "url": "/images/f7735ba2-6c6a-485a-b2aa-bdeade9ea2bc_rw_3840.jpg",
              "w": 3840,
              "h": 2160
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Searches through hardware stores and online forums led me to an experimental 3D printing material called LAYFOMM, a combination of a porous substance and PVA. The printing process is carried out as usual, but the real magic happens when the printed object is immersed in water. Over the course of up to two days, the PVA, which gave the object its rigidity, dissolves, leaving behind the same form, but now more flexible and, most importantly, capable of absorbing liquids. The guiding concept is to print the material, transform it into a sponge, soak it in liquid porcelain, dry it, and fire it. After firing, the only remaining material is the porcelain."
        }
      ],
      "displayTitle": "Impossible Ceramics",
      "cover": "/images/0a965397-c172-45d4-86be-51f4149d3bde_carw_16x9x5120.jpg"
    },
    {
      "slug": "construction-design-for-moria-architects",
      "title": "Parametric fabrication plan Tel Aviv Museum of Art",
      "modules": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In collaboration with Moria Architects & OBA studio, I developed a parametric fabrication plan for a 500 m² topographic installation exhibited at the Tel Aviv Museum of Art. Using my script, I was able to easily convert any solid shape to ready-to-cut, automatically numbered DXF files. The hills were designed by Moria Architects, and I used Grasshopper to translate their concept into a buildable system. The structure is made of birch wood, with OBA Studio in charge of construction and assembly. \n Exhibition's museum web page"
        },
